ise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Let's break this down.\n\n**Hand:**  \n- Hole cards: Ac, 9s  \n- Community cards: Ah, 8d, Kd, As, Qs  \n\n**Hand evaluation:**  \nThe board has: Ah, As, Qs, Kd, 8d.  \nYou have two aces in your hand (Ac + board's Ah and As), so you have three aces.  \nYour best 5-card hand: Ah, As, Ac, Kd, Qs (three aces + K and Q kickers).  \n\nThat’s a strong **Three of a Kind**, top trips with good kickers (King and Queen on board).  \n\n**Game state:**  \n- Final betting round (River complete).  \n- You’ve raised every street preflop, flop, and turn, opponent has just called each time.  \n- Opponent’s possible holdings: Could have an ace with a weaker kicker (maybe Ax with x < K/Q), could have two pair (Aces and Kings, but then they’d have AAKKQ, which beats your three aces only if they have AK specifically), could have a straight draw that missed (board: no straight possible except if they had JT for Broadway, but JT doesn’t match their passive calls unless they were chasing).  \n- They might also have a worse ace (A8, AQ, maybe even a set of kings if they had KK preflop, but unlikely given they didn’t reraise preflop).  \n\n**Pot size reasoning:**  \nStart stack each hand = 20,000.  \nYour current stack = 19,350, so you’ve put in 650 total (200 preflop + 100 flop + 300 turn).  \nOpponent called those, so pot now = 650 × 2 = 1,300 chips.  \n\n**Your valid actions:**  \nCheck (0), raise any amount ≥100 (up to 19,350), all-in (-2), fold (-1).  \n\n**Considerations:**  \n1. **Check (0)**: Lets opponent bluff if they’re weak, but you likely have the best hand. Checking could lose value if they’d call a small bet.  \n2. **Bet small (100)**: Minimum raise option; might get called by worse aces or even kings.  \n3. **Bet medium**: Something like 500–1,000 could look like a value bet and still get called.  \n4. **All-in (-2)**: Overbet (pot 1,300, you shove ~19,350). This will likely only get called if you’re beat (AK, AQ, maybe a full house if opponent has AA/KK/A8? But AA and KK unlikely; A8 would’ve flopped two pair, now full house with board’s third ace). Unlikely they call with worse given the massive size.  \n\nGiven opponent has been passive (just calling), they might call a medium bet with an Ace worse than King kicker, or maybe even with a king (KQ, KJ). But if they have AQ they’ve got Aces with Q kicker, same as you except their Q kicker is paired on board — actually, wait: board is Ah As 8d Kd Qs.  \n- If they have AQ: Their hand = Ah As Ac Qs Qs (board’s Qs) — that’s full house (Aces full of Queens). That beats your three aces.  \nThey’d need exactly AQ or 88 or KK for a better hand. KK unlikely because no preflop 3-bet? Possible but they might have slowplayed. 88 unlikely because board has 8d only; if they had 88 they’d have flopped a set, but still only three 8s, not full house. Actually with Ah As 8d Kd Qs, 88 would be 8d 8x plus Ah As Qs Kd? That’s still just three 8s, not full house. So better hand possibilities:  \n    - AA (unlikely, you have Ac, board has Ah As, so only one Ace left in deck and they’d have it — very rare)  \n    - AQ (makes Aces full of Queens)  \n    - QsQs (Quads? impossible, Qs is on board)  \n    - KK (makes Kings full of Aces)  \n\nGiven they just called all streets, they could be slowplaying a monster.  \n\nBut if they had AQ or KK, they’d likely have raised at some point with such a strong hand on turn or river because board is drawy earlier.  \n\nProbably you’re ahead most of the time.  \n\n**Best play:**  \nBet medium for value. Small bet 100 is too small relative to pot 1,300. Maybe bet ~700 (about half pot).  \nAll-in is bad—kills action when ahead, gets called only when behind.  \n\nSince valid actions let you raise any amount >100, I’d choose a value bet of 700.  \n\n**Evaluation:**  \nYou likely have the best hand (three aces with K and Q kickers). Opponent’s passive calling suggests they have a weaker ace or maybe a marginal hand like two pair (AK? but then they’d have AAKKQ, that’s two pair, which loses to your three aces). So value bet is correct.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nOpponent has shown no aggression; likely they’ll call a moderate bet with worse. All-in will fold out worse hands and only get called by better. Checking could miss value.  \n\n**Answer:** 700  \n\n---\n\nEvaluation: You have three aces with strong kickers, likely ahead of opponent’s range given their passive calls.  \nReason: A moderate value bet extracts chips from weaker aces or two pairs while not scaring them off.  \nAnswer: 700"}
